---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONFIG } from '../config';
---
<BaseLayout title="About · Astra3" description="What Astra3 is, why it exists, its development philosophy and its relationship to PyMOL." current="about">
<header class="page-head"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>Why Astra3 exists</h1></div></header>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>Astra3, the Structural Analysis Toolkit for PDB Rendering Automation, is a desktop application that automates the PyMOL work structural biologists repeat for every structure: fetching, cleaning, colouring, measuring, rendering and reporting.</p>
    <h2>Scientific motivation</h2>
    <p>Hand-running the same commands invites small inconsistencies: a chain coloured differently, a selection made slightly differently, a value copied from the wrong run. Astra3 applies the same logic every time and writes down what it did, so results can be compared, checked and reproduced.</p>
    <h2>Development philosophy</h2>
    <p>Astra3 is built to be a reliable structural analysis assistant. It reports values read from PyMOL, computed from coordinates, or parsed from the entry's own records. When something is unavailable, the report says so, and close contacts are reported as geometry.</p>
    <h2>Relationship to PyMOL</h2>
    <p>Astra3 does not replace PyMOL. It requires your own installation, runs its analysis through it, and saves a standard PyMOL session you can open and keep working in.</p>
    <h2>Open source</h2>
    <p>Astra3 is released under the MIT License. Releases, the licence and citation metadata are on <a href={CONFIG.githubUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>. Created by Andre Aguirre · <a href={CONFIG.linkedinUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>.</p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
